  "tips": "## Passive Participles (Pa'ul Pattern)\n\nThe **pa'ul** (פעול) pattern -- **CaCuC** -- turns a verb root into a passive participle. It describes a state resulting from an action:\n\n| Root | Active meaning | Pa'ul form | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| כ-ת-ב | to write | **כתוב** | written |\n| ס-ג-ר | to close | **סגור** | closed |\n| פ-ת-ח | to open | **פתוח** | open |\n| ש-ב-ר | to break | **שבור** | broken |\n| י-ד-ע | to know | **ידוע** | known |\n| ג-נ-ב | to steal | **גנוב** | stolen |\n\n### Agreement\nLike adjectives, pa'ul forms agree in gender and number:\n- סגור / סגורה / סגורים / סגורות\n- שבור / שבורה / שבורים / שבורות\n\n### אסור and מותר\nTwo very common participles used as impersonal expressions:\n- **אסור** (forbidden) + infinitive: אסור לעשן -- It's forbidden to smoke\n- **מותר** (allowed) + infinitive: מותר להיכנס? -- Is it allowed to enter?\n\nThese don't change form -- they are always used impersonally:\n- אסור לרוץ -- It's forbidden to run\n- מותר לשבת -- It's allowed to sit\n\n### Describing States\nPa'ul participles describe states, not actions:\n- הדלת **סגורה** -- The door is closed (state)\n- הספר **כתוב** בעברית -- The book is written in Hebrew\n- הטלפון **גנוב** -- The phone is stolen"
